+1. Introduction
+With the rapid advancement of the Internet and the
+widespread adoption of mobile devices, the amount of
+available information and content has increased dram
+atically. In particular, as markets in various service
+domains such as e-commerce, streaming services, an
+d social media continue to expand, the overwhelming
+abundance of products and content has made it incre
+asingly difficult for users to identify items that best
+match their preferences. In response to this challeng
+e, recommender systems have emerged as a technolo
+gy that assists users in making appropriate choices.
+A recommender system is a type of Information Filte
+ring technology that aims to improve user satisfactio
+n by recommending information that users are likely
+to find interesting [1,2,3,4]. Therefore, recommender
+systems contribute to alleviating the problem of infor
+mation overload while providing significant benefits t
+o both users and service providers.
+The two most important factors in recommender sys
+tems are training speed and recommendation accurac
+y. Training speed refers to how quickly a recommen
+der system can learn from newly acquired data and
+provide users with appropriate recommendations in r
+eal time. Since user preferences and behavioral patter
+ns change rapidly over time, a recommender system
+that fails to promptly reflect these changes may prov
+ide outdated recommendations, thereby reducing user
+satisfaction. In addition, recommendation accuracy is
+an important metric that indicates how relevant and
+appropriate the recommended items are for individual
+users. A highly accurate recommender system effecti
+vely captures user preferences and delivers personali
+zed content [5,6,7,8,9,10].
+To address these issues, this study proposes a novel
+method that improves the performance of a Graph Si
+gnal Processing (GSP)-based recommender system
+by incorporating user rating information. Convention
+al Graph Convolution Network (GCN)-based recomm
+ender systems effectively model complex graph struc
+tures through graph convolution operations. Howeve
+r, they require a large number of trainable parameter
+s, and the optimization process involves substantial c
+omputational cost and long training time [11,12,13,14,
+15]. As a result, updating the recommender system
+model is time-consuming. To overcome this limitatio
+n, Graph Signal Processing-based recommender syst
+ems have been introduced as an efficient alternative
+for modeling relatively simple relationships [16,17,18,
+19,20,21].
+Graph Signal Processing-based recommender syste
+ms perform recommendation through a low-pass filte
+r, which consists of simple matrix operations and doe
+s not require the learning of trainable weight paramet
+ers. Consequently, the model can be updated efficient
+ly with significantly lower computational overhead. F
+urthermore, their recommendation accuracy is compa
+rable to or even higher than that of Graph Convolutio
+n Network-based recommender systems [18,19,20,2
+1].
+However, existing Graph Signal Processing-based r
+ecommender systems treat items with high user rati
+ngs and those with low user ratings equally without
+distinguishing between them when constructing the
+model [21]. Therefore, this study proposes a method
+that incorporates users' high rating information into
+the model to achieve more personalized recommendat
+ions.
+2. Related Works
+Recommender systems are generally classified into
+Content-Based Filtering, Collaborative Filtering, and
+Hybrid Filtering, which combines the strengths of bo
+th approaches [1,2,3,4].
+Content-Based Filtering recommends items with cha
+racteristics similar to those previously preferred by t
+he user by utilizing item attributes. Although it can
+generate recommendations without relying on other
+users' data, it is limited by the difficulty of extractin
+g item features and its inability to fully capture users
+' diverse interests.
+Collaborative Filtering is based on the assumption th
+
+=== 3쪽 ===
+Performance Improvement of Recommendation Systems using User Evaluation Information
+at users with similar preferences tend to favor simila
+r items [3,5,6,8,9,10,22]. It is categorized into User-Ba
+sed and Item-Based Collaborative Filtering [7]. This
+approach does not require item feature information a
+nd can effectively discover users' potential interests.
+However, it suffers from the Cold Start Problem, the
+Data Sparsity Problem, and increasing computational
+complexity as user-item interactions grow.
+Hybrid Filtering combines Content-Based and Colla
+borative Filtering to improve recommendation perfor
+mance. It can alleviate the Cold Start Problem while
+enhancing recommendation diversity, although it intr
+oduces higher model complexity and computational c
+ost.
+With the advancement of deep learning-based Collab
+orative Filtering, Graph Convolution Networks (GCN
+s) have been widely applied to recommender systems
+[11]. Representative models include NGCF [12], Light
+GCN [13], SimGCL [14], and XSimGCL [15].
+Graph Signal Processing (GSP) extends conventiona
+l signal processing to graph-structured data by explo
+iting graph topology [16,17]. Its core concepts include
+Graph Signals, the Graph Laplacian Matrix, Graph Fi
+ltering, and the Graph Fourier Transform (GFT). In
+particular, Graph Filtering transforms graph signals i
+nto the frequency domain, where Low-pass Filters s
+uppress high-frequency components while preservin
+g the similarities among neighboring nodes.
+GF-CF (Graph Filter-based Collaborative Filtering)
+is a representative recommender system based on Gr
+aph Signal Processing [18]. To capture user-item si
+milarities, GF-CF employs both Linear Low-pass an
+d Ideal Low-pass Filters. Subsequently, several impr
+oved models were proposed, including PGSP, which
+incorporates richer user information [19], Turbo-CF,
+which improves computational efficiency [20], and Hi
+GSP, which introduces hierarchical user clustering be
+fore graph filtering [21].
+This study is based on HiGSP, which achieved state
+-of-the-art performance among Graph Signal Proces
+sing-based recommender systems as of 2024. HiGSP
+combines a cluster-based filtering module with a glo
+bal-aware filtering module consisting of an Ideal Lo
+w-pass Filter and a High-order Low-pass Filter to
+effectively model user preferences and predict future
+interactions.
+A previous study [18] compared the Graph Convoluti
+on Network-based model LightGCN with the Graph
+Signal Processing-based model GF-CF. Since GF-C
+F operates solely through matrix operations without
+trainable parameters, it achieves significantly higher
+computational efficiency while maintaining comparabl
+e or even superior recommendation performance on t
+he Yelp2018 and Amazon-book datasets. Motivated b
+y these advantages, this study investigates whether
+incorporating user rating information can improve th
+e recommendation quality of a HiGSP-based framew
+ork.
+3. The Proposed Scheme
+Existing Graph Signal Processing-based recommend
+er systems, including HiGSP, construct the User Inte
+raction Matrix for building the adjacency matrix by
+considering only whether an interaction exists betwe
+en a user and an item, without taking user rating inf
+ormation into account [18,19,20,21]. This approach is
+based on the assumption that users interact more fre
+quently with items they prefer. However, in real-wor
+ld scenarios, users may repeatedly interact with item
+s to which they assign low ratings, while interacting
+less frequently with items they rate highly. Conseque
+ntly, an adjacency matrix constructed solely from int
+eraction information may fail to adequately reflect us
+ers' actual preferences, potentially leading to degrade
+d recommendation performance.
+To address this limitation, this study proposes a met
+hod that additionally incorporates user rating informa
+tion during the construction of the User Interaction
+Matrix. Specifically, an observed user-item interactio
+n is included in the rating-based interaction matrix o
+nly when its rating is greater than or equal to a thres
+hold N. In the results reported in Section 4, N = 2 an
+
