+This paper reports the performance of Ratings2 and
+Ratings5 on four benchmark datasets: ML-100K, ML
+-Latest-Small, ML-1M, and CiaoDVD. Compared wi
+th HiGSP, Ratings2 improves 23 of the 24 reported d
+ataset-metric comparisons, while Ratings5 improves
+all 24 comparisons. Nevertheless, Ratings2 exhibits a
+slight decrease in MRR@10 on ML-1M. On CiaoDV
+D, Ratings5 achieves larger improvements at K = 5,
+whereas Ratings2 achieves larger improvements at
+K = 10. These findings indicate that incorporating rat
+ing information can improve recommendation quality,
+but the benefit depends on the dataset, rating thresho
+ld, combination weights, and evaluation metric.
+This study builds upon our previous conference wor
+k [25] by further examining the rating-aware graph
+filtering approach for recommendation systems. Alth
+ough the proposed method improves recommendation
+performance in several evaluation metrics, its use of
+two adjacency matrices introduces additional comput
+ational overhead compared with the original HiGSP
+model. In particular, independently processing the int
+eraction-based and rating-based adjacency matrices
+requires additional graph construction, filtering, and a
+ggregation operations. This represents a trade-off be
+tween recommendation accuracy and computational e
+fficiency. Because execution time and memory consu
+mption were not measured in the reported experimen
+ts, the magnitude of this trade-off remains an open
+question. As future work, we plan to investigate a un
+ified rating-weighted adjacency matrix that incorpor
+ates both interaction information and user preference
+s into a single graph structure. This approach aims
+to reduce redundant filtering operations while preser
+ving the recommendation performance achieved by t
+he proposed method.
