+and 14.46% in NDCG@5. At K = 10, however, Rating
+s2 yields higher scores than Ratings5, with relative
+improvements of 8.24% in F1@10, 11.03% in MRR@1
+0, and 9.31% in NDCG@10. Therefore, neither thresh
+old is uniformly superior across all evaluation metric
+s, and the preferred setting depends on the recomme
+ndation cutoff and evaluation objective.
+According to Table 1, CiaoDVD has the lowest inter
+action density among the reported datasets, at appro
+ximately 0.026%. One possible interpretation is that t
+he rating-based branch provides a more selective pre
+ference signal, while the interaction-based branch ret
+ains the broader interaction structure. However, the
+present results do not establish that sparsity is the c
+ause of the improvement. The large relative gains m
+ust also be interpreted in the context of the low basel
+ine scores. For example, the 16.54% improvement in
+MRR@5 for Ratings5 corresponds to an absolute incr
+ease from 0.0133 to 0.0155, or 0.0022. The relative per
+centage gains and the absolute score differences sho
+uld therefore be considered together when interpretin
+g the results on CiaoDVD.
